{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Grouping Symbols.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Grouping Symbols.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"grouping symbols grouping symbols","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Grouping Symbols.md","fileName":"Grouping Symbols.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Grouping Symbols","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Algebraic Expressions]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Algebraic Expressions","Suppression of Parentheses","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Grouping Symbols.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Grouping Symbols.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"grouping symbols grouping symbols","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Grouping Symbols.md","fileName":"Grouping Symbols.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Grouping Symbols","heading":"Grouping Symbols","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Grouping symbols such as parentheses (( )), brackets ([ ]), and braces ({ }) specify which part of an expression is treated as a unit. Operations inside the innermost group are evaluated before operations acting on the group.\n\nGrouping can override ordinary precedence and preserve the intended base of a power. For example, ((3+2)^2=25), while (3+2^2=7). Removing symbols is safe only when the surrounding operation permits it. A preceding negative sign changes every term inside the group through [[Suppression of Parentheses]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Algebraic Expressions","Suppression of Parentheses","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Grouping Symbols.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Grouping Symbols.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"grouping symbols grouping s